/target/
# cargo-mutants writes its run directory here (and rotates the previous one to
# `mutants.out.old`). Tool output, never repository content — the useful part of a run is the
# conclusion recorded in a PR or in BACKLOG.md, not the artifacts.
/mutants.out/
/mutants.out.old/
# Each example is its own workspace; its build artifacts and resolved lockfile are local only. The
# examples commit the adopter's caret dependency form, like the in-repo fixtures, and carry no lock:
# a committed one would pin the family to whatever was published when it was generated, so an
# example run without the examples gate's local-source patch would demonstrate a release other than
# the tree it was cloned from. The version is deliberately not named here — it lives in each
# example's own manifest, and naming it twice is how this comment went stale once already.
/examples/*/target/
/examples/*/Cargo.lock
# The in-repo fixtures are their own workspaces too, and one of them now carries a dependency — the
# shell-to-metadata edge the self-law fixture exists to violate. The reaction reads them with
# `cargo metadata --no-deps`, which resolves nothing and writes no lock, but any ordinary command that
# does resolve (`cargo build`, `cargo tree`, a bare `cargo metadata`) leaves one behind. Untracked and
# unignored, that file makes `crates/kanhe/tests/publish_source.rs` refuse: it reads
# `--untracked-files=all` and a
# dirty worktree means `HEAD` no longer describes what would be packaged — so a test artifact would
# block an irreversible act. Committing one is not the alternative; a fixture's resolved lock is local
# only, exactly as an example's is.
/crates/*/tests/fixtures/*/target/
/crates/*/tests/fixtures/*/Cargo.lock
